      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "A longitudinal follow-up of Modic changes on magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess the prevalence and natural course Modic changes over a 3-year follow-up period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Modic changes are bone marrow and endplate lesions visible on MRI. To the authors' knowledge, no follow-up studies on their natural course have been published."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study population consisted of 60 unoperated sciatica patients 23 to 76 years of age. Baseline and 3-year lumbar MR images from L1-L2 through L5-S1 were analyzed independently by 2 radiologists and a consensus reading was performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At baseline, the prevalence of Modic changes was 23%. Seven discs had mixed Type I/II, and 63 Type II change. Changes typically occurred at L4-L5 and L5-S1, and associated positively with age (P = 0.009). Ten of 70 discs (14%) with Modic changes at baseline displayed another type at 3 years. Furthermore, the nonconverted changes increased significantly in size. The incidence of new Modic changes during the follow-up was 6% (13 of 230)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Modic changes are common MRI findings in patients with degenerative lumbar disc disease. We found evidence that Modic Type II changes may be less stable than previously assumed."
        }
      ]},
